From: z060142 <z060142@yahoo.com.tw>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 15:49:10 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 164/174] feat: capture-side film grain, stage G0 - skeleton,
 gating and the white-Gaussian generator

The engine's film grain is an overlay of a 256x256x6 white-noise volume, pivoting on 0.5,
addressed in FRAME coordinates and animated off the wall clock: six patterns cycling at 18 Hz,
monochrome, no size, no tone response, and grain cells that double in pixels when the resolution
doubles. This is the first stage of replacing it, for the cinematic camera only, with a
capture-side model (FilmGrainSpec.md).

What the new grain is. Grain has a size in MICROMETRES ON THE NEGATIVE, so the same camera gives
the same grain at 1080p and at 4K and a smaller sensor shows coarser grain relative to the frame.
Its amplitude follows the tone through a response curve that peaks in the mid-tones and falls to
zero at display white and nine stops under it. Each emulsion layer gets its own field, mixed by a
correlation, so the grain carries colour speckle rather than sitting on the picture as one grey
layer. And the pattern is a pure function of (shot seed, capture frame index, view, film-space
cell, layer) - no clock anywhere - so the same shot reproduces the same grain in any session, at
any frame rate, and the grain changes every CAPTURED frame instead of eighteen times a second.

Renderer

- Grain_User_* post-effect params at manager level (PostProcess.cpp/.h): Active, Family, and the
  Vec4s Amount / Size / Sensor / Seed / Digital0 / Digital1, plus the two texture-id slots
  Response and Plates that G1 and G2 will fill. All no-transition - a grain parameter is a
  property of the negative, not a value to cross-fade - and all inert by default.
- r_FilmGrain (1), r_FilmGrainDebug (0) and r_FilmGrainFreeze (0) in RendererCVars, in the house
  style of r_DepthOfFieldLensModel.
- CPostAAStage::DoFinalComposition reads the block once per frame. When the camera asks for it,
  the cvar allows it and the amount is non-zero, it forces the stock grain amount to 0, sets
  %_RT_SAMPLE6 (the one free runtime flag on this technique) and uploads the seven vectors through
  the pass' existing reflected per-primitive constant buffer. The flag travels in rtMask, which is
  already part of the pass IsDirty tuple, so the permutation change rebuilds the primitive; no new
  texture is bound, so nothing else joins the tuple. The freeze latch holds the published capture
  frame index while r_FilmGrainFreeze is on without stopping the camera's own count.
- r_FilmGrainDebug 3 prints a once-a-second plain-language report - family, per-channel amount,
  size in um and in output pixels, what one output pixel covers of the negative, seed and frame
  index, and what the grain is worth in 8-bit code values on a mid grey card, which is the number
  that decides whether it is visible at all after the 8-bit write.

Shader

- New Engine/Shaders/HWScripts/CryFX/FilmGrain.cfi, included by PostAA.cfx and called from the
  stock grain's own slot before the saturate under %_RT_SAMPLE6. It brings its own pcg3d integer
  hash and Box-Muller Gaussian, because there is no hash, PRNG or blue-noise anywhere in
  PostEffectsLib.cfi / ShadeLib.cfi / Common.cfi and 5.7.1 ships no blue-noise texture.
- The blend is the spec's: decode sRGB, t = log2(x), g = amount * R(t), x' = x * exp2(g*n -
  g*g*ln2/2), encode. The second exponent term is the analytic Jensen correction, so raising the
  grain does not also raise the exposure. Above the clip point g is 0 and the pixel is returned
  untouched rather than round-tripped through the encoding, which keeps clipped highlights and the
  letterbox bars bit-identical.
- Coordinates are film millimetres with the anamorphic squeeze undone on x, so grain stretches
  with a desqueezed frame. The G0 generator is a white Gaussian on a film-space cell grid with the
  output pixel's footprint integrated analytically (variance / max(1, (fp/size)^2)) - zero assets,
  and it is the shot-noise core of the sensor model that comes next, so it is not throw-away.
- r_FilmGrainDebug 1 replaces the picture with a mid grey card and runs the whole block on it.
- The stock ApplyFilmGrain is untouched and is still what compiles with the flag clear, so every
  stock permutation is byte for byte what it was.

Cinematic camera

- SCineGrainParams becomes the spec's property group: Family (Film / CMOS / CCD / Phone), Grain
  Strength, Size, Colour, Shot Seed, Capture Frame Rate and Camera Rolling While Paused, next to
  the existing Enable Film Grain.
- ApplyFilmGrain publishes the whole block every frame under CanDriveRenderState(), saved once and
  restored on release under the existing grain flag, and keeps writing FilterGrain_Amount so
  r_FilmGrain 0 lands straight back on the engine's grain at the same strength.
- Amount = ComputeFilmGrain01() * Grain Strength * the family's per-layer amplitude (Film: B 1.3 /
  G 0.9 / R 1.0, correlation from the Colour property; the digital families are neutral until the
  sensor model lands). Size is 6 um times the Size property, with the film layer radii B 1.25 /
  G 0.8 / R 1.0. Sensor width and squeeze come from the body and the anamorphic group.
- The capture-frame counter of section 5.3: one step per finalised game-camera frame, reset when
  the component takes authority and when Shot Seed changes, held while paused unless Camera
  Rolling While Paused. The TrackView sequence-time mapping is a TODO in AdvanceCaptureFrame -
  no hook on this component reports the playing sequence's time, so Capture Frame Rate is stored
  but not yet consulted.

Docs: README.md's Film Grain property table and film grain model section, ConsoleReference.md's
three new cvars and the Grain_User_* param block.

